  "id": "Compound::DB00741 - Disease::DOID:8577",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the treatment of ulcerative colitis through its interaction with the corticosteroid receptor. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, where Hydrocortisone is classified within the broader category of corticosteroid hormone receptor agonists, which includes betamethasone and triamcinolone. Both betamethasone and triamcinolone are known to treat ulcerative colitis, suggesting a mechanistic link between corticosteroid receptor activation and the alleviation of inflammatory symptoms in this condition. The shared ancestry of Hydrocortisone, betamethasone, and triamcinolone further reinforces this connection, as they all belong to the same class of compounds with similar biological activities.\n\nThe evidence also suggests that Hydrocortisone may contribute to the pathogenesis of proximal myopathy, a condition that is indirectly linked to ulcerative colitis through the metabolic and inflammatory pathways influenced by corticosteroid receptor activation. While the direct mechanism by which Hydrocortisone exerts its therapeutic effect on ulcerative colitis remains to be fully elucidated, the consistent presence of corticosteroid receptor agonist activity in the treatment of the disease supports a mechanistic framework involving receptor-mediated signaling. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for hydrocortisone to be repurposed for the treatment of ulcerative colitis, provided further experimental validation of its specific mode of action is obtained.",
